# /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/71-iceland-dma-heap.rules (iceland-device)
#
# Keep the desktop's programs off the CMA dma-heaps; they keep the system heap.
#
# The CMA area of kernel-iceland is 32 MiB (stock's linux,cma is 36 MiB), exported as the heaps "reserved" and
# "default_cma_region". libcamera's software ISP allocates its output buffers from the first heap it can open and
# tries the CMA heaps before the system heap. Four 1920x1080 RGBA buffers (4 x 8.3 MB) do not fit: GNOME Camera's
# stream fails ("dma-heap allocation failure for frame-3", then PipeWire "Format negotiation failed",
# area-combo-46). The system heap needs no contiguous memory and the GPU and PipeWire take its buffers as well.
#
# Fedora's 70-libcamera.rules gives every dma_heap node to the group "video" (0660); this rule runs after it and
# takes the CMA heaps back to root. Programs running as root (and the kernel's own users of CMA) are not affected.
SUBSYSTEM=="dma_heap", KERNEL!="system", GROUP="root", MODE="0600"
#
# The system heap itself for the group "video" (0660), as 70-libcamera.rules does today (libcamera 0.7.1 iceland4
# ships it): stated here as well, so the cameras keep working if a later libcamera package drops or changes that rule
# (tb323fu-linux, same SoC, 70-tb323fu-dma-heap.rules: without the system heap camera apps find no camera). The same
# result as before on the current packages.
SUBSYSTEM=="dma_heap", KERNEL=="system", GROUP="video", MODE="0660"
